{{!-- ENCOUNTERS — what has been gathered for one session or one scene, in named bundles: the
      monsters, the read-aloud page, the battle map, the treasure table, the arcanum somebody is
      about to find. The first of the Prep tab's three views (gm-toolkit-tab-prep.hbs), and the
      one it opens on. With Expeditions, the panes on this sheet that hold POINTERS to other
      documents rather than prose about them.

      A PANE OF THE PREP TAB'S OWN GROUP, `data-group="prep"`, not the rail's: the switcher at
      the top of that tab shows and hides it. `tab encounters` is still its class and that is not
      a leftover — `.tab.encounters` is the scope every listener and the drop zone in
      gm-encounters-tab.js is bound to, and the scope its edit flush searches for a focused field.
      Rename it and the pane draws perfectly and does nothing.

      TWO LISTS, ONE CARD. What is still to come is at the top; what has been run is filed into
      the Completed fold at the foot, and the tick on a card is what sends it there. Both lists
      print `gm-encounter-card.hbs`, which is where everything about a single encounter lives —
      including the one button that reads differently in the two places. The split itself is made
      in JS off each encounter's `used` flag (gm-encounters-tab.js), never off the DOM.

      NOTHING IS LOST BY COMPLETING ONE. A filed encounter keeps its notes, its collected rows,
      its Deploy button and its dropzone, and one press of the arrow puts it back on the live
      list. That is the whole reason the trash is a separate button behind a pencil: the frequent
      act is reversible and one click, and the final one asks first.

      WRITES DO NOT GO THROUGH THE FORM. Every field here is handled by gm-encounters-tab.js and
      saved straight to `actor.system.encounters`, so none of them carries a `name` attribute: a
      `name` on an input inside this sheet's <form> puts it in core's submit data, where the array
      indices would expand into an OBJECT keyed "0", "1", ... and quietly replace the list with it.

      ONE PENCIL for the whole pane, beside the bar's New button (stonetop.css anchors it there),
      and it guards exactly the two delete buttons — in BOTH lists, because the `--reading` class
      sits on the <section> that wraps them. Everything else stays live while it is off, which is
      the point rather than an oversight: the reason this tab exists is to be dropped into and noted
      on mid-session, and a tab that has to be unlocked first is one nobody uses. What the pencil is
      FOR is that "delete this encounter" is one press on the head of a shut row, and now the ONLY
      button on that head: a lone 22px square, at the end of a row a GM drags things onto and types
      in constantly, whose one press throws a session's prep away.

      NOT `stonetop-readonly`. That class is the sheet-wide lock (pointer-events off every input,
      textarea and button, then an allow-list), and on this tab it would put out exactly the fields
      the paragraph above says have to stay lit. `--reading` hides the two buttons it is about and
      touches nothing else.

      `data-tab="encounters"` is also what earns the switcher segment its stack glyph (the
      `.stonetop-gm-prep-view[data-tab]` rows in stonetop.css). The rail no longer has a button
      by this name, so nothing in the rail's own icon table answers to it. --}}
<div class="tab encounters steading-edit-section{{#if stonetop.prep.panes.encounters.active}} active{{/if}}" data-group="prep" data-tab="encounters"
     id="{{stonetop.prep.panes.encounters.id}}" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="{{stonetop.prep.panes.encounters.tabId}}">
	{{> "stonetop.steading-section-toggle" section="encounters" editing=stonetop.edit.encounters}}
	<section class="sheet-tab stonetop-gm-encounters{{#unless stonetop.edit.encounters}} stonetop-gm-encounters--reading{{/unless}}">

		{{!-- THE PANE BAR: a one-line lead, the "?" that opens the full intro under it, and "New
		      encounter" at the right. On a view with nothing in it at all the button moves into the empty state
		      below, larger, and the bar is drawn `bare` (gm-prep-pane-bar.hbs says why). --}}
		{{> "stonetop.gm-prep-pane-bar" kind="stonetop-gm-encounter"
		    lead=(localize "stonetop.gmToolkit.encounters.lead")
		    intro=(localize "stonetop.gmToolkit.encounters.intro")
		    pane=stonetop.prep.panes.encounters
		    label=(localize "stonetop.gmToolkit.encounters.add")
		    bare=(not (or stonetop.encounters.active.length stonetop.encounters.completed.length))}}

		{{!-- THE LIVE LIST CARRIES NO HEADING OF ITS OWN. It is what the pane IS, directly under
		      the pane's own bar, and a "Prepared" heading over it would only be there to make a
		      pair with the fold below — which is rendered at all only once something has been
		      run. --}}
		{{#if stonetop.encounters.active.length}}
		<ul class="stonetop-gm-encounter-list">
			{{#each stonetop.encounters.active}}
			{{> "stonetop.gm-encounter-card"}}
			{{/each}}
		</ul>
		{{else if stonetop.encounters.completed.length}}
		{{!-- Two empty states, because "nothing here yet" and "you have run everything you
		      prepared" are different facts and the second one is wrong advice: there is no need
		      to tell a GM to drag something in when what they most likely want is to lift one
		      back out of the fold two inches below. This one is a quiet line, not the centred
		      block: the pane is not empty, its fold is right there. --}}
		<p class="stonetop-gm-encounter-empty">{{localize "stonetop.gmToolkit.encounters.emptyAllDone"}}</p>
		{{else}}
		{{> "stonetop.gm-prep-empty" view="encounters" kind="stonetop-gm-encounter"
		    title=(localize "stonetop.gmToolkit.encounters.emptyTitle")
		    text=(localize "stonetop.gmToolkit.encounters.empty")
		    label=(localize "stonetop.gmToolkit.encounters.add")
		    accepts=stonetop.encounters.accepts}}
		{{/if}}

		{{!-- COMPLETED, and rendered only when there is something in it, so an untouched pane shows
		      one list and not two.

		      It folds, and starts OPEN like every other section on this sheet: a section shut on
		      first open is one a GM never learns is there. Folding it is a choice the caret offers
		      and the sheet then remembers per user.

		      ITS HEADING IS AN EYEBROW, not a section title: small caps, the count beside it (the
		      heading's `note`, styled as a count in stonetop.css), and a hairline to the edge — the
		      same line the open card's groups are named with, one size up. A history at the foot of
		      the pane should not shout as loud as the work above it.

		      The caret rides ON THE HEADING (via `section-heading`) rather than in a corner,
		      because this section has no pencil of its own: the pane's single pencil already
		      reaches the delete buttons inside here, and a second one would be two controls for
		      one lock. `stonetop-move-group-title` stays on it because that is what the sheet's
		      fold wiring counts as a heading (HEADING_SELECTOR in StonetopGmToolkitSheet.js).

		      The heading's FOLLOWING SIBLINGS are what the fold hides (`_sectionFoldTargets`), so
		      the box around this pair is load-bearing: without it the walk would run on past the
		      list and out of the tab. Nothing else may go in this box, and a SECOND heading in it
		      would stop the caret claiming at the first. --}}
		{{#if stonetop.encounters.completed.length}}
		<div class="stonetop-gm-encounters-completed">
			{{> "stonetop.section-heading" title=(localize "stonetop.gmToolkit.encounters.completed")
			    note=stonetop.encounters.completed.length
			    headingClass="stonetop-move-group-title stonetop-gm-fold-eyebrow"
			    collapse="gm-encounters-completed"}}
			<ul class="stonetop-gm-encounter-list stonetop-gm-encounter-list--completed">
				{{#each stonetop.encounters.completed}}
				{{> "stonetop.gm-encounter-card"}}
				{{/each}}
			</ul>
		</div>
		{{/if}}

	</section>
</div>
